{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The claim needs a country and time period to be assessed accurately. It is true in some countries and periods, but not universally.\n\nFor example, historical U.S. data have often shown lower average life expectancy for Black Americans than for the population overall. The size of that gap varies by year and sex.\n\nWhere a gap exists, it does not establish that race itself causes shorter lives. Life expectancy reflects factors such as income, healthcare access, environmental conditions, discrimination, and exposure to violence. A population average also does not predict an individual’s lifespan."}}
